---
// oRPC's story, from its own write-up (2026-10-08); the page counts, dates,
// and dependencies are checked against the public middleapi/orpc repository
// and its migration pull request, and the Lighthouse scores are oRPC's own
// runs.
import { NEW_TAB_ATTRS } from "blume/core/new-tab.ts";
import StoryPage from "../_customers/StoryPage.astro";
import { storyById } from "../_customers/customers.ts";
---

<StoryPage story={storyById("orpc")}>
  <p>
    <a href="https://orpc.dev" {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>oRPC</a> is an open-source
    framework for building APIs that are typesafe end to end, with OpenAPI
    built in. Developers write procedures, or start from a contract, and get a
    fully typed client, an OpenAPI spec, and schemas from Zod, Valibot, or
    ArkType. Around that core, oRPC integrates with Next.js, TanStack Query,
    NestJS, the AI SDK, Cloudflare Workers, OpenTelemetry, and more.
  </p>
  <p>
    Its docs cover all of it, for developers who read the code first. 44 pages
    use <a href="/docs/content/syntax#display-types">Twoslash</a>, so readers
    can hover over any token in a code example and see the type the compiler
    infers.
  </p>

  <h2>The challenge</h2>
  <p>
    Those examples are why oRPC's docs live in its monorepo. The docs are a
    package there, built against 14 of oRPC's own packages from the
    workspace, so every Twoslash example checks against the code in the repo,
    including changes that haven't been released yet. The team wanted the
    polish of a full docs platform, but the docs had to stay next to the
    packages.
  </p>
  <p>
    VitePress handled that well. But Twoslash, Mermaid diagrams, and llms.txt
    each came from a plugin, and as AI features mattered more to the team, the
    list kept growing. Each new feature was another dependency to set up and
    keep current.
  </p>

  <h2>The move</h2>
  <p>
    Blume runs as a normal package inside a repo, so the docs could stay where
    they were. Định Lê, oRPC's maintainer, started the move on August 5, 2026,
    and merged it the next day, less than 24 hours later, in{" "}
    <a href="https://github.com/middleapi/orpc/pull/1793" {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}
      >one pull request</a
    >.
  </p>
  <p>
    All 96 pages moved to MDX, and none changed its URL. VitePress's
    containers became Blume's <code>:::</code> directives, code groups became
    tabs, collapsible details became expandables, shared snippets were
    inlined, and the sidebar's order carried over into{" "}
    <a href="/docs/content/navigation#ordering"><code>meta.ts</code></a> files.
    The Twoslash blocks and code annotations came across unchanged.
  </p>
  <p>
    The docs package got lighter, too. VitePress, Vue, Mermaid and the layout
    libraries it needs, and the plugins for Twoslash, diagrams, and llms.txt,
    16 dev dependencies in all, were replaced by one: <code>blume</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    What had been plugins came built in, and some features came along that
    the team hadn't asked for: an Open Graph card for every page, a sitemap,
    and content signals in robots.txt.
  </p>

  <h2>Built around the code</h2>
  <p>
    oRPC's readers search for the API they're using, so the team made code
    searchable. An{" "}
    <a href="/docs/configuration/customization#astro-integrations"
      >Astro integration</a
    > in <code>blume.config.ts</code> adds code blocks to the search index, and the
    search dialog previews each match as it appears on the page. Others anchor
    Twoslash popups beside their token, swap in oRPC's own header, and place
    sponsor slots through the docs.
  </p>
  <p>
    The theme takes oRPC's pink as its accent, with a dark mode based on the
    Zed editor's One Dark palette.
  </p>

  <h2>Ready for agents</h2>
  <p>
    AI features were a big part of why oRPC moved, and now they're defaults or
    a line of config. The site publishes{" "}
    <a href="/docs/discoverability/llms-txt">llms.txt</a> and llms-full.txt,
    which oRPC links from its header, and a{" "}
    <a href="/docs/discoverability/mcp">docs MCP server</a> answers at{" "}
    <code>/mcp</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Blume also{" "}
    <a href="/docs/discoverability/agent-discovery#skills-discovery"
      >publishes</a
    > the agent skills from the monorepo's <code>skills</code> folder, so a coding
    agent finds oRPC's four skills, for contracts and OpenAPI among them, on the
    docs site itself.
  </p>

  <h2>The results</h2>
  <p>
    On October 8, 2026, the oRPC team ran Lighthouse on mobile against{" "}
    <code>/docs/getting-started</code>, on VitePress and on Blume. Each score
    is the median of three runs, and the Blume page also loaded 76% less
    JavaScript.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Lighthouse, mobile</th>
        <th scope="col">VitePress</th>
        <th scope="col">Blume</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Performance</th><td>48</td><td>76</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Accessibility</th><td>88</td><td>97</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Best Practices</th><td>100</td><td>100</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">SEO</th><td>100</td><td>100</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The move kept every URL and every Twoslash example, and the docs section
    has grown from 91 pages to 122 since. The docs are live at{" "}
    <a href="https://orpc.dev" {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>orpc.dev</a>, and their
    source is{" "}
    <a
      href="https://github.com/middleapi/orpc/tree/main/apps/content"
      {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>public on GitHub</a
    >. The v1 docs are still at{" "}
    <a href="https://v1.orpc.dev" {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>v1.orpc.dev</a>, and
    redirects send the v1 URLs search engines still index to their v2 pages.
  </p>
  <p>
    To make the same move, follow the{" "}
    <a href="/guides/migrate-from-vitepress">VitePress migration guide</a>.
  </p>
</StoryPage>
